{
  "id": "str-fiscal-quarter-boundary",
  "number": 119,
  "title": "Fiscal Quarter Labels Across The Year Boundary",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "tags": [
    "Date"
  ],
  "description": "Same fiscal year as before (starts April 1st), but now report each employee's full fiscal quarter label, e.g. `FY2021-Q4` for a hire in January 2022 (fiscal Q4 of FY2021, since FY2021 runs April 2021 through March 2022).\n\nName the columns `name` and `fiscal_quarter`. Return the result in any order.",
  "solutionSql": "SELECT name,\n  'FY' || (CASE WHEN CAST(strftime('%m', hire_date) AS INTEGER) >= 4\n           THEN CAST(strftime('%Y', hire_date) AS INTEGER)\n           ELSE CAST(strftime('%Y', hire_date) AS INTEGER) - 1 END)\n  || '-Q' ||\n  (CASE\n     WHEN CAST(strftime('%m', hire_date) AS INTEGER) BETWEEN 4 AND 6 THEN 1\n     WHEN CAST(strftime('%m', hire_date) AS INTEGER) BETWEEN 7 AND 9 THEN 2\n     WHEN CAST(strftime('%m', hire_date) AS INTEGER) BETWEEN 10 AND 12 THEN 3\n     ELSE 4\n   END) AS fiscal_quarter\nFROM employees;",
  "orderMatters": false,
  "hint": "The fiscal year part uses the same Jan-Mar-rolls-back-a-year logic as before. The quarter number is independent of that: Apr-Jun is always fiscal Q1, regardless of which calendar year it happened to fall in.",
  "outputExplanation": "Ava (Jan 2018) gets FY2017-Q4, Eve (Nov 2017) gets FY2017-Q3, and Dan (Jul 2021) gets FY2021-Q2. Each employee's label reflects the fiscal year rules and which quarter their hire month falls in.",
  "schemaSql": "CREATE TABLE departments (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT,\n  location TEXT\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE employees (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT,\n  title TEXT,\n  salary INTEGER,\n  dept_id INTEGER,\n  manager_id INTEGER,\n  hire_date TEXT\n);",
